/*
RMSTEST_22b - elevation seed south bias, WITH enable_balanced_elevation
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 1, Sec.6.2's seed weights

QUESTION
Identical to RMSTEST_22a in every respect except the one attribute, which is
the only way to attribute a difference to it. The guide still calls the
distribution "slightly biased" even with the attribute set, so Sec.6.2 models
the residual as 100 + 15*y/(dim-1), a 1.15x pull instead of 2x. The 15 is the
softest [tune] number in the whole document. It was chosen to be small and
nonzero because the guide says "slightly", and that is the entire evidence.

Three outcomes are all live, and they are not variations on a theme:
  mean y near 0.512   the residual bias is real and roughly the modelled size
  mean y near 0.500   the attribute removes the bias entirely, and the guide's
                      "slightly" describes sampling noise rather than a rule
  mean y near 0.556   the attribute does nothing at all, which would be a
                      finding of the same shape as item 9's set_gaia_object_only

The third is worth stating out loud because a preview that models a knob the
engine ignores lies in exactly the direction users trust most, and because this
project has already found one attribute that did nothing.

METHOD, GENERATION SIZE, COVERAGE CAUTION AND PLAYER LAND REASONING
All as RMSTEST_22a. Read that header. Nothing here differs except the
attribute, and keeping the two identical is the point.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. mean y sits strictly below RMSTEST_22a's, whatever the absolute value. If it
   does not, the attribute is inert.
2. mean y lands between 0.500 and 0.525.
3. mean x lands on 0.500 within noise, same control as 22a.

RUN IT THREE TIMES, and run it in the same sitting as 22a so the comparison is
not across a game patch.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --rows ELEVATION
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  enable_balanced_elevation

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 500
    number_of_tiles 2000
    spacing 1
  }


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
